{
  "teamId": 141,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Toronto Blue Jays were a team that looked the part on paper — talented, balanced, and hungry after consecutive postseason appearances — but spent much of the year fighting to prove it. They burst out of the gate in April with a 17-10 record, powered by electric moments like Bo Bichette's go-ahead three-run blast early in the month. But the bullpen, particularly closer Jordan Romano, showed troubling cracks almost immediately, surrendering late leads that would become a recurring theme throughout the season.\n\nMay became the nadir of the year, the month-by-month record cratering to 11-17 as Romano and the relief corps repeatedly coughed up leads in the late innings. A J.T. Realmuto double off Romano on May 10th and a barrage of opposing home runs off the bullpen told the story of a team that could not hold advantages when it mattered most. Yet even in the darkness, Danny Jansen's clutch single on May 14th — plating Vladdy and Chapman in a massive moment — showed the offense was capable of rising to the occasion.\n\nThe Jays steadied themselves through June, July, and August, posting winning records in three of those four months. Vladimir Guerrero Jr. and Cavan Biggio provided timely power, Danny Jansen continued to flash heroics behind the plate, and the rotation anchored by Kevin Gausman kept the team afloat. The September stretch drive saw Toronto surge to 16-11, fueled by memorable clutch plays from Daulton Varsho, Matt Chapman, and Whit Merrifield, ultimately securing an 89-win season and a Wild Card berth.\n\nThe postseason, however, was brief and brutal. Facing the Minnesota Twins in the Wild Card round, Toronto was undone by the player who had haunted them all season in different forms: Royce Lewis homered off Gausman in Game 1, and in the decisive Game 2, a Matt Chapman double play in a loaded-bases situation extinguished the Blue Jays' best chance to rally. The Twins eliminated Toronto in two games, leaving a talented roster with a hollow finish and questions that would echo into the offseason.",
